{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Of all food that may be eaten.</b> This refers back to the upper clause: \"whatever is inside it shall become unclean — of all food that may be eaten\" — upon which water has come and it is inside an unclean earthen vessel, it shall become unclean (and likewise any liquid that may be drunk, in any vessel, if it is inside an unclean earthen vessel, shall become unclean). From here we learn many things: we learn that food is not susceptible and prepared to receive impurity until water has come upon it once, and once water has come upon it once it is susceptible to impurity forever, even if dry; and wine and oil and everything called a liquid prepare seeds for impurity like water — for thus the verse is to be expounded: that upon which water comes, or any liquid that may be drunk in any vessel — the food becomes unclean. And our Sages further learned from here that an offspring of impurity does not defile vessels, for thus we learned (Pesachim 80): I might think all vessels become defiled from the air of an earthen vessel — Scripture says: \"whatever is inside it shall become unclean, of all food\" — food (and liquid) becomes defiled from the air of an earthen vessel, but all vessels do not become defiled from the air of an earthen vessel — because the creeping thing is a father of impurity, and the vessel defiled from it is an offspring of impurity; therefore it does not turn and defile the vessels inside it. And we further learn that a creeping thing that fell into the air of an oven and the bread is inside it and the creeping thing did not touch the bread — the oven is first-degree and the bread is second-degree; and we do not say we view the oven as if it were full of impurity and the bread would be first-degree — for if you say so, no vessels would be exempt from defilement from the air of an earthen vessel, since impurity itself touched them from above. And we further learn about the application of water that it does not prepare seeds unless it falls upon them after they have been detached — for if you say they receive preparation while still attached, you would have nothing upon which water has not come; so what is the meaning of \"upon which water comes\"? And we further learn that food does not defile others unless it contains the volume of an egg, as it says \"that may be eaten\" — food eaten in one swallowing, and the Sages estimated that the gullet holds no more than a hen's egg (Sifra; Yoma 80)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:59.688503685Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}